1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,750
Imagine you are standing
in a huge car park.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:05,470
There are hundreds of cars around you.

3
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:08,370
And someone says: "Take the red car."

4
00:00:09,119 --> 00:00:10,319
Pretty straightforward.

5
00:00:10,819 --> 00:00:15,549
But what if they said: "Take the car
red." That sounds strange in English.

6
00:00:15,939 --> 00:00:17,649
But you would still understand it.

7
00:00:18,309 --> 00:00:20,289
So why do some languages put adjectives

8
00:00:20,369 --> 00:00:22,959
before the noun, while
others put them after?

9
00:00:23,469 --> 00:00:25,879
And is one of those
systems actually better?

10
00:00:28,379 --> 00:00:30,369
In English, we say: a red car.

11
00:00:30,939 --> 00:00:32,509
The adjective comes first.

12
00:00:33,299 --> 00:00:35,769
The same basic pattern appears in German.

13
00:00:36,189 --> 00:00:37,677
Ein rotes Auto.

14
00:00:38,126 --> 00:00:39,448
And in Swedish.

15
00:00:39,528 --> 00:00:44,517
En röd bil. And in Norwegian. En rød bil.

16
00:00:44,967 --> 00:00:47,257
But then we have languages such as French.

17
00:00:47,997 --> 00:00:49,247
Une voiture rouge.

18
00:00:49,327 --> 00:00:51,397
Literally: a car red.

19
00:00:52,264 --> 00:00:54,194
Spanish does something similar.

20
00:00:54,513 --> 00:00:57,434
Un coche rojo. A car red, again.

21
00:00:58,371 --> 00:01:02,201
And this is not some odd
exception. It is the majority.

22
00:01:03,311 --> 00:01:06,201
Linguists have surveyed more
than thirteen hundred languages.

23
00:01:07,153 --> 00:01:10,643
In eight hundred and seventy-nine
of them, the noun comes first.

24
00:01:11,553 --> 00:01:14,443
In three hundred and
seventy-three, the adjective does.

25
00:01:15,251 --> 00:01:18,281
So the order English uses
is the rarer of the two.

26
00:01:19,031 --> 00:01:20,621
Most languages say car red.

27
00:01:21,685 --> 00:01:26,915
And then there is Polish. Polish can do
something rather interesting. For example.

28
00:01:27,595 --> 00:01:29,663
Czarne buty turystyczne.

29
00:01:30,114 --> 00:01:31,844
Literally: black boots tourist.

30
00:01:32,574 --> 00:01:36,364
So you have got one adjective before
the noun, and another one after it.

31
00:01:37,060 --> 00:01:38,530
And it is not random.

32
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:41,830
Black is just a description,
so it goes in front.

33
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:45,410
Hiking is what the boots
are for, so it goes behind.

34
00:01:46,333 --> 00:01:51,043
Polish basically says: "Why choose?"
And that turns out to be quite useful.

35
00:01:53,673 --> 00:01:55,163
So which way is better?

36
00:01:55,573 --> 00:01:59,323
At first glance, there is a good
argument for putting the noun first.

37
00:02:00,192 --> 00:02:04,122
Consider: "the car red." The
first thing you hear is car.

38
00:02:04,572 --> 00:02:07,082
So immediately, you know
what we are talking about.

39
00:02:07,908 --> 00:02:13,358
Then the adjective simply adds
information. The car... red. The car...

40
00:02:13,568 --> 00:02:16,018
old. The car... enormous.

41
00:02:16,762 --> 00:02:19,332
This is useful when the
object itself is new.

42
00:02:20,322 --> 00:02:22,972
Imagine someone says:
"There is a car outside."

43
00:02:24,028 --> 00:02:25,938
Now you know what we are talking about.

44
00:02:26,288 --> 00:02:29,178
And then: "It is red."
Perfectly straightforward.

45
00:02:30,184 --> 00:02:34,414
The noun gives the listener something to
attach the rest of the information to.

46
00:02:35,102 --> 00:02:37,532
Everything that follows
is just adding detail.

47
00:02:38,402 --> 00:02:43,372
So perhaps putting the noun first is
more efficient. Problem solved. Except...

48
00:02:43,862 --> 00:02:45,062
not really.

49
00:02:47,272 --> 00:02:49,142
Let us go back to that car park.

50
00:02:49,852 --> 00:02:51,072
There are two hundred cars.

51
00:02:51,152 --> 00:02:53,242
And I say: "Take the car."

52
00:02:54,028 --> 00:02:57,498
Thanks. Very useful. I
already knew I needed a car.

53
00:02:57,868 --> 00:02:59,398
The problem is: which one?

54
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,840
Now imagine I say: "Take
the red car." Suddenly,

55
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:06,410
red is the important piece of information.

56
00:03:07,452 --> 00:03:09,162
"Car" tells you almost nothing.

57
00:03:09,812 --> 00:03:13,142
"Red" lets you actually find
the thing you are looking for.

58
00:03:13,865 --> 00:03:15,875
And that changes the whole argument.

59
00:03:16,865 --> 00:03:19,185
Because if we already know
what we are talking about,

60
00:03:19,265 --> 00:03:22,275
then giving us the noun
first can be a waste of time.

61
00:03:23,008 --> 00:03:25,298
The adjective may be
the more useful piece.

62
00:03:26,088 --> 00:03:28,688
So red car can be more
efficient than car red,

63
00:03:28,768 --> 00:03:31,458
because the important
information arrives first.

64
00:03:34,158 --> 00:03:36,028
And this is where it gets interesting.

65
00:03:37,078 --> 00:03:40,288
Perhaps the question is not:
"Which word order is better?"

66
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:43,210
Perhaps the real question is: "Which

67
00:03:43,290 --> 00:03:45,920
information does the
listener already have?"

68
00:03:46,726 --> 00:03:49,916
If we are introducing something
new, the noun is important.

69
00:03:51,086 --> 00:03:55,216
But if we are standing next to a row
of cars, the noun is almost redundant.

70
00:03:56,189 --> 00:03:58,239
We already know we are talking about cars.

71
00:03:59,109 --> 00:04:03,309
What we need is the information that
tells one car from another. The colour.

72
00:04:03,389 --> 00:04:05,499
The size. The age. Whatever.

73
00:04:07,829 --> 00:04:10,739
And this is something we forget
when we talk about grammar.

74
00:04:11,529 --> 00:04:13,599
Language does not happen in isolation.

75
00:04:14,839 --> 00:04:19,409
We do not normally walk around describing
unknown objects to people with no context.

76
00:04:20,399 --> 00:04:21,789
We share the same room.

77
00:04:22,399 --> 00:04:23,889
We can see the same things.

78
00:04:24,795 --> 00:04:26,865
We have already been
talking about the subject.

79
00:04:27,755 --> 00:04:31,805
We have previous sentences. We
have gestures. We have context.

80
00:04:32,975 --> 00:04:36,085
And all of that changes which
words actually carry information.

81
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:39,280
In information theory,
something the receiver

82
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:42,410
already knows carries very
little new information.

83
00:04:43,420 --> 00:04:46,070
And language works in a
surprisingly similar way.

84
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:51,710
There is another argument
for putting the noun first.

85
00:04:52,440 --> 00:04:56,270
Imagine this: "The large new
red German electric sports..."

86
00:04:57,206 --> 00:05:01,536
And you are still waiting. What? A
car? A motorcycle? A boat? An aircraft?

87
00:05:02,593 --> 00:05:07,283
English can build impressively long noun
phrases where the listener has to wait

88
00:05:07,413 --> 00:05:12,103
until the very end to discover what all
those adjectives are actually describing.

89
00:05:13,036 --> 00:05:15,046
Putting the noun first solves that.

90
00:05:15,756 --> 00:05:19,496
"The car — large, new, red,
German, electric, sporty..."

91
00:05:19,576 --> 00:05:22,226
Now you know what we are talking
about from the beginning.

92
00:05:23,075 --> 00:05:25,425
So noun first has a real advantage.

93
00:05:26,415 --> 00:05:29,395
But in ordinary conversation,
context usually means

94
00:05:29,475 --> 00:05:32,125
we do not have to introduce
everything from scratch.

95
00:05:33,072 --> 00:05:36,362
And that is why adjective
first can be extremely useful.

96
00:05:39,082 --> 00:05:40,832
Which brings us back to Polish.

97
00:05:41,442 --> 00:05:44,772
Czarne buty turystyczne.
Black boots, tourist.

98
00:05:45,702 --> 00:05:49,792
English would normally put everything
before the noun. Black hiking boots.

99
00:05:50,688 --> 00:05:53,438
Norwegian does both,
but not with adjectives.

100
00:05:53,988 --> 00:05:55,938
Those stay in front, like English.

101
00:05:56,668 --> 00:06:00,958
It moves the possessive instead.
Bilen min. The car mine.

102
00:06:01,568 --> 00:06:03,718
Swedish and Danish keep that in front.

103
00:06:04,532 --> 00:06:09,622
And that is a nice reminder that languages
do not have to choose one universal rule.

104
00:06:10,492 --> 00:06:13,502
They can use different positions
for different kinds of information.

105
00:06:14,508 --> 00:06:16,448
Which brings us to a
language that takes this

106
00:06:16,528 --> 00:06:19,118
idea in a rather more creative direction.

107
00:06:21,838 --> 00:06:25,878
French. Because just when you think
you have worked out the rules, French

108
00:06:25,958 --> 00:06:29,548
decides that the position of an
adjective can change its meaning.

109
00:06:30,502 --> 00:06:33,092
Take: Un grand homme.

110
00:06:33,282 --> 00:06:35,962
This means, roughly, a
great or important man.

111
00:06:36,042 --> 00:06:38,222
But: Un homme grand.

112
00:06:38,302 --> 00:06:40,212
means a tall man.

113
00:06:41,067 --> 00:06:45,017
Same adjective. Same noun. Different
position. Different meaning.

114
00:06:47,187 --> 00:06:49,867
And there is more. Un pauvre homme.

115
00:06:49,947 --> 00:06:52,917
can mean an unfortunate or pathetic man.

116
00:06:53,717 --> 00:06:58,287
Whereas: Un homme
pauvre. means a poor man.

117
00:06:58,557 --> 00:07:01,427
As in, someone who does
not have much money.

118
00:07:02,191 --> 00:07:05,421
From an English or Czech point of
view, this feels slightly unfair.

119
00:07:06,451 --> 00:07:08,811
If I say black shoes, or, for some reason,

120
00:07:08,891 --> 00:07:11,821
poetically, shoes black,
they are still black shoes.

121
00:07:12,678 --> 00:07:14,038
But French says: "Ah.

122
00:07:14,118 --> 00:07:17,578
You have moved the adjective."
And suddenly: "No, no.

123
00:07:17,658 --> 00:07:19,528
We meant something completely different."

124
00:07:20,371 --> 00:07:23,601
The position of the adjective
has become part of the meaning.

125
00:07:24,591 --> 00:07:26,611
It is almost as if the
language had hidden an

126
00:07:26,691 --> 00:07:29,541
extra piece of information
in the word order.

127
00:07:30,304 --> 00:07:33,194
And honestly... that is
a little bit naughty.

128
00:07:36,264 --> 00:07:37,594
So what is the verdict?

129
00:07:38,364 --> 00:07:42,324
After all this, you would probably expect
us to declare a winner. Noun first?

130
00:07:42,404 --> 00:07:44,194
Or adjective first?

131
00:07:45,044 --> 00:07:47,394
But there is not really
a universal winner.

132
00:07:48,304 --> 00:07:50,944
Noun first has a very
nice property: "I will

133
00:07:51,024 --> 00:07:53,094
tell you what we are talking about first."

134
00:07:53,910 --> 00:07:56,530
Adjective first has another:
"I will tell you what

135
00:07:56,610 --> 00:07:59,840
makes it different first."
And both are useful.

136
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:05,360
And perhaps this is the
most interesting part of it.

137
00:08:06,130 --> 00:08:09,000
Languages are not perfectly
engineered systems.

138
00:08:09,887 --> 00:08:14,757
Nobody sat down thousands of years ago,
designed English, and said: "Right.

139
00:08:15,407 --> 00:08:17,567
Adjectives go before nouns.

140
00:08:17,647 --> 00:08:20,497
This should maximise
communicative efficiency."

141
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:23,600
Languages evolved. People used them.

142
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:25,050
People changed them.

143
00:08:25,500 --> 00:08:28,170
Some patterns became
common. Others disappeared.

144
00:08:29,178 --> 00:08:32,328
And different languages ended
up making different compromises.

145
00:08:33,058 --> 00:08:38,428
English, German, Swedish and Czech
generally put adjectives before nouns.

146
00:08:39,004 --> 00:08:42,494
French, Spanish and many other
languages often put them afterwards.

147
00:08:43,424 --> 00:08:45,414
Polish can happily mix the two.

148
00:08:46,164 --> 00:08:49,034
And French can even use the
position to change the meaning.

149
00:08:51,994 --> 00:08:53,764
So which one is actually better?

150
00:08:54,454 --> 00:08:56,344
Perhaps the answer is: it depends.

151
00:08:57,362 --> 00:09:00,612
When the noun is new information,
noun first can be useful.

152
00:09:01,722 --> 00:09:06,312
When the noun is already obvious from
context, adjective first can be better.

153
00:09:07,081 --> 00:09:10,041
And that means the supposedly
simple question — should

154
00:09:10,121 --> 00:09:14,011
we say red car or car red —
turns into a much bigger one.

155
00:09:14,686 --> 00:09:18,146
"What information does the
listener need right now?" And

156
00:09:18,226 --> 00:09:21,176
that is a question every
human language has to solve.

157
00:09:21,846 --> 00:09:23,696
They just solve it in different ways.

158
00:09:26,176 --> 00:09:28,256
So the next time someone
tells you "take the

159
00:09:28,336 --> 00:09:30,926
red car," you can appreciate the grammar.

160
00:09:31,819 --> 00:09:34,379
But more importantly, you
will know exactly why they

161
00:09:34,459 --> 00:09:36,869
did not bother telling
you to take the car first.

162
00:09:37,539 --> 00:09:39,239
There are two hundred of them.

163
00:09:39,319 --> 00:09:41,569
And you already know they are cars.
